A||N||1||starter248||Stall problem.. Please help!||||5||
Z||000000||starter248||10-23-2000||12:35 AM||||Hello and thanks again,<P>I got some tips for this problem but they haven't panned out, I replaced the throttle position sensor and it didn't fix the problem. Check out the original message below, let me know your thoughts, I'm deperate at this point. <P><BR>Here's my original messsage:<BR>-----------Start Original--------------------<BR>Hi all, newcomer here looking for some help. I have an 86SE V6 with an 88 Formula engine, auto transaxle. It runs very smooth while crusing but has an intermittent problem with stalling when coming to a stop and resuming travel. Symptoms are as follows, high idle in park @ 2000rpm and sometimes slows to a rough idle which may be @ 500rpm, when starting off from a complete stop I must let the car "idle" off the line otherwise when you if you give it gas before the car gains enough speed it stalls. I was told this could have something to do with the TCC solenoid, however I have disconnected the TCC which I was told should fix this problem but it has not. Interestingly enough the problem occured a couple of times then ceased for several weeks and now it's back and doesn't seem to want to go away. This is a dangerous problem as I could get nailed buy someone when I stall so I would appreciate any suggestions. Thanks much,<BR>Scott<P>-----------End Original----------------------<P>I thought the TPS actually helped because I was able to gun it from a dead stop all the way to 60mph with no misses, many successive times without stalling   Then, to my displeasure, it started f&%$ing up again and I'm back to square one. In addition to the TPS I have replaced the EGR Tube/Valve within the last month as well as using a single bottle of injector cleaner fuel additive.  Also note that when it does die the battery voltage gage seems very low, less than 12 for sure.  When crusing at highway speed the voltage seems to be just fine, at least 12 if not 13 or slightly more.  I'm not really mechanically inclined, so it's definitely new territory for me. This is the first car I've really had the desire to work on because it is such a great piece of art. Once again, all input would be greatly appreciated. Thanks again,<P>Scott||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||10-23-2000||01:19 AM||||Couple things to try:<P>1)Disconnect and plug vacuum hose to EGR.<P>2)Unplug tach filter.<P>3)Disconnect battery positive terminal.  Check/tighten (gently!) connections at the starter.  If + batt. terminal is icky, clean it and the - batt. terminal.  Reconnect battery.<P>4)Remove/inspect/replace each spark plug wire.  Run motor in darkness, and look for arcking. If you have any doubts about their condition and can afford it, go ahead and replace the spark plug wires with a new set. <P>After each of the above steps, test drive car and note its behavior.  By the way, even if item 1) cures the stalling, do <B>not</B> plan on leaving the EGR disconnected   <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-23-2000).]||||reg||3
